1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,201
A business pays a supplier for years.

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:06,769
Then one email says the bank details have changed.

3
00:00:06,769 --> 00:00:12,877
Horos is an agent that pays invoices on Arc, and it cannot be talked
into paying that new address.

4
00:00:12,877 --> 00:00:19,347
Not because it spots the lie, but because naming a destination is not
something the contract can be asked to do.

5
00:00:19,970 --> 00:00:27,120
Business email compromise cost three point zero four billion dollars
last year, across twenty four thousand incidents.

6
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:31,067
The usual control is to phone the supplier and confirm.

7
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:38,893
That control is now under attack: deepfake voice and video
impersonation of vendors was measured for the first time in twenty

8
00:00:38,893 --> 00:00:46,614
Every channel a business uses to learn a new address can be
synthesised, so Horos does not try to tell a real voice from a fake

9
00:00:47,230 --> 00:00:52,375
Here is the entire safety argument, and it fits in one function
signature.

10
00:00:52,325 --> 00:00:56,674
pay takes a counterparty id, an amount, and a reference.

11
00:00:56,674 --> 00:00:59,225
There is no to parameter.

12
00:00:59,225 --> 00:01:02,559
The caller names who to pay, never where.

13
00:01:02,559 --> 00:01:11,686
The destination is read from a registry, and the only way an address
becomes a counterparty's active account is a ceremony the recipient

14
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:17,210
This is the live deployment on Arc mainnet, reading real USDC.

15
00:01:17,159 --> 00:01:22,159
A routine invoice releases: the address is the one already on record.

16
00:01:22,159 --> 00:01:29,114
Now one arrives from the same supplier, with a changed account,
urgency, and an instruction not to verify.

17
00:01:29,114 --> 00:01:34,009
The policy holds it, and it names the sentence that gave the document
away.

18
00:01:34,609 --> 00:01:37,073
The site reads two chains.

19
00:01:37,023 --> 00:01:41,424
Mainnet runs in real USDC, signed by a local key.

20
00:01:41,424 --> 00:01:46,332
Testnet runs the same contracts keyless - Circle holds the only key.

21
00:01:46,332 --> 00:01:52,165
The page says which one you are reading, because that difference is a
custody claim and not a setting.

22
00:01:52,769 --> 00:01:57,394
When a supplier genuinely changes accounts, that is a ceremony.

23
00:01:57,344 --> 00:02:01,055
The account that was last paid agrees to be replaced.

24
00:02:01,055 --> 00:02:06,314
The business agrees, with a key named at registration that nobody else
can use.

25
00:02:06,314 --> 00:02:09,961
The new account can never sign for its own arrival.

26
00:02:09,961 --> 00:02:15,338
The lineage is public, and it is not editable: every entry is a
transaction.

27
00:02:15,949 --> 00:02:19,233
None of that requires trusting the website.

28
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:24,001
This reads the deployment straight from the chain, with no key and no
API.

29
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:29,274
Two accounts, the second replacing the first, with two signatures
behind it.

30
00:02:29,274 --> 00:02:36,278
And the interface, checked rather than asserted: pay takes a
counterparty id, and has no address parameter.

31
00:02:36,899 --> 00:02:43,333
Horos is live on Arc mainnet, and that record was made by a real
ceremony in real USDC.

32
00:02:43,283 --> 00:02:51,317
The repository has the contracts, an adversarial audit with every fix
behind a test, and the same policy the deployed site runs.

33
00:02:51,317 --> 00:02:53,921
The refusal is the product.

